"Building Faster Cryptography with Carryless Multiplication in NVIDIA CUDA 13.3 | NVIDIA Technical Blog (https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/building-faster-cryptography-with-carryless-multiplication-in-nvidia-cuda-13-3/)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/building-faster-cryptography-with-carryless-multiplication-in-nvidia-cuda-13-3/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 146\nL0: 【0†DEVELOPER】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Technical Blog】\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Search blog] [Input] [Input]\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Trustworthy AI / Cybersecurity】\nL7: \nL8: [Select]\nL9: \nL10: # Building Faster Cryptography with Carryless Multiplication in NVIDIA CUDA 13.3\nL11: \nL12: 【3†Image: Decorative image.†developer-blogs.nvidia.com】\nL13: \nL14: Jul 15, 2026\nL15: \nL16: By 【4†Jean-Luc Watson】, 【5†Charles Gouert】, 【6†Michael B. Sullivan】 and 【7†G. Edward Suh】\nL17: \nL18: [Button: Like ]\nL19: \nL20: 【8†Discuss (0)】\nL21: ## AI-Generated Summary\nL22:   * NVIDIA CUDA 13.3 introduces clmad, a hardware-accelerated PTX instruction for carryless multiply-accumulate on all Ampere and newer GPUs (SM 80+).\nL23:   * GHASH throughput reaches ~6.3 TB/s on the NVIDIA B200, up to 18.8x faster than the prior bitsliced state of the art and close to DRAM read bandwidth.\nL24:   * Sum-check protocol performance over GF(2^128) improves by 4–13x versus the previous bitsliced approach, with gains scaling alongside polynomial and composition sizes.\nL25:   * Carryless multiplication underpins a wide range of cryptographic and coding workloads including CRC, Reed–Solomon, BCH codes, quantum stabilizer codes, post-quantum schemes, and binary-field zero-knowledge systems such as Binius.\nL26: ### Next Steps\nL27: \nL28:   * Download 【9†CUDA 13.3】 to access the clmad instruction.\nL29:   * Explore the 【10†cuPQC SDK】 for post-quantum cryptographic workloads.\nL30:   * Review the 【11†NVIDIA Aerial documentation】 for telecom applications using carryless multiplication.\nL31: \nL32: Powered by NVIDIA Nemotron. AI-generated content may summarize information incompletely. Verify important information. 【12†Learn more†www.nvidia.com】\nL33: For over fifteen years, x86 CPUs have shipped with a dedicated hardware instruction for carryless multiplication. It’s a small but stubborn primitive that sits underneath authenticated encryption, error-correcting codes, and modern zero-knowledge proofs.\nL34: Until now, NVIDIA GPUs lacked native support for this operation. NVIDIA CUDA 13.3 closes that gap with `clmad`, a new 【13†PTX instruction†docs.nvidia.com】 available on all NVIDIA Ampere and newer GPUs (SM 80+). In this post, we show what is possible when carryless multiplication is finally a hardware-accelerated GPU primitive.\nL35: We benchmark two cryptographic workloads that depend on it: GHASH, the integrity hash inside AES-GCM (the AEAD cipher behind TLS, VPN, and most data-center encryption), and the sum-check protocol, the workhorse inner loop of advanced zero-knowledge proving systems. On the NVIDIA B200, GHASH throughput reaches ~6.3 TB/s—close to DRAM read bandwidth and up to 18.8x faster than the prior bitsliced state of the art. Sum-check over \\(GF(2^{128})\\) speeds up the prior state of the art by 4–13×.\nL36: Why does this matter beyond AES-GCM? Carryless multiplication is the shared kernel underneath a surprisingly wide range of cryptographic and coding-theoretic workloads. CRC and Reed–Solomon codes used in storage systems and telecom baseband processing, BCH codes for flash memory, quantum stabilizer codes, several post-quantum cryptographic schemes, and the binary-field arithmetic that underpins modern zero-knowledge proving systems such as Binius.\nL37: Hardware acceleration on the GPU changes the cost structure for all these workloads, on every Ampere-or-later system already deployed.\nL38: This post is written for CUDA developers integrating cryptography into GPU pipelines and for security and privacy researchers building on binary-field protocols. 【9†Download CUDA 13.3】 to follow along; the inline PTX examples below can drop straight into your kernels.\nL39: # Binary extension fields\nL40: \nL41: The smallest finite field, \\(GF(2)\\), is just a single bit, where additions are XORs and multiplications are ANDs. In cryptographic use cases, bits are typically combined into binary extension fields, \\(GF(2^n)\\), where \\(n\\) bits represent the binary coefficients of a polynomial over \\(GF(2)\\) and operations are taken modulo an irreducible polynomial.\nL42: Polynomial addition in the field is still a simple XOR (element-wise coefficient addition), but multiplication requires a long multiplication between the bits of each input.\nL43: \nL44: Specifically, the \\(i\\)’th resulting coefficient of an extension-field multiplication of inputs \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is \\(\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{i} a_j b_{i-j}\\) – the XOR of individual bit products, followed by a reduction by the field’s irreducible polynomial.\nL45: For example, the 2-bit extension field \\(GF(2^2)\\) has one possible irreducible polynomial, \\(X^2 + X + 1\\).\nL46: It’s possible to emulate this computation as a series of AND and XOR operations, but the repeated bitshifts and masking required to extract individual bit values result in significant overhead. Direct hardware support for this type of field multiplication has been available in 【14†x86†www.intel.com】, primarily supporting 【15†AES-GCM†cdrdv2-public.intel.com】.\nL47: However, until now, carryless multiplication hasn’t been available on NVIDIA GPUs, requiring developers to adopt alternative techniques like 【16†bitsliced circuits†www.irreducible.com】 to improve performance.\nL48: # Hardware support for carryless multiplication\nL49: \nL50: CUDA 13.3 adds new PTX support for a carryless multiply-accumulate instruction, 【17†`clmad`†docs.nvidia.com】.\nL51: Much like 【14†PCLMULQDQ†www.intel.com】 in x86, `clmad` performs a carryless multiplication of two 64-bit inputs into a 128-bit result. `.hi` and `.lo` variants calculate the top and bottom halves, respectively, of the 128-bit output with the addition of a 64-bit accumulator, as shown:\nL52: \nL53:     __device__ inline uint128_t clmad_mul_128(uint64_t a, uint64_t b, uint128_t acc) {\nL54:     \tuint64_t acc_lo = (uint64_t)acc;\nL55:     \tuint64_t acc_hi = (uint64_t)(acc >> 64);\nL56: \nL57:     \tuint64_t lo, hi;\nL58:     \t// Inline PTX: calculate lower and higher 64 bits of result\nL59:     \tasm(\"clmad.lo.u64 %0, %1, %2, %3;\" : \"=l\"(lo) : \"l\"(a), \"l\"(b), \"l\"(acc_lo));\nL60:     \tasm(\"clmad.hi.u64 %0, %1, %2, %3;\" : \"=l\"(hi) : \"l\"(a), \"l\"(b), \"l\"(acc_hi));\nL61: \nL62:     \treturn ((uint128_t)hi << 64) | lo;\nL63:     }\nL64: This instruction is accelerated in hardware on NVIDIA GPUs since Ampere (`sm_80` or higher) for quick binary extension field multiplication on modern NVIDIA GPUs.\nL65: In the remainder of this post, we look at how `clmad` can accelerate two cryptographic use cases – GHASH and the sumcheck protocol–both of which operate in the larger \\(GF(2^{128})\\) field. In that field, elements can be multiplied using the 【18†Karatsuba algorithm†en.wikipedia.org】 in 6 `clmad` instructions:\nL66: \nL67:     __device__ inline uint256_t clmad_mul_256(uint128_t a, uint128_t b) {\nL68:     \t// Isolate low and high halves of inputs\nL69:     \tuint64_t a0 = (a & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFull), a1 = (a >> 64);\nL70:     \tuint64_t b0 = (b & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFull), b1 = (b >> 64);\nL71: \nL72:     \t// Karatsuba: divide-and-conquer with smaller 64x64->128 bit carryless mults\nL73:     \tuint128_t z0 = clmad_mul_128(a0, b0, 0);\nL74:     \tuint128_t z1 = clmad_mul_128(a0 ^ a1, b0 ^ b1, 0);\nL75:     \tuint128_t z2 = clmad_mul_128(a1, b1, 0);\nL76:     \tz1 ^= z0 ^ z2;\nL77:     \tz0 ^= z1 << 64;\nL78:     \tz2 ^= z1 >> 64;\nL79:     \treturn {z2, z0}; // to be reduced back to GF(2^128)\nL80:     }\nL81: To get back to a 128-bit field from the 256-bit result, polynomial reduction can be computed either by a multiplication-based 【19†Barrett reduction†en.wikipedia.org】 with additional CLMAD calls or by a direct shift-and-XOR loop that emulates polynomial long-division.\nL82: # GHASH acceleration\nL83: \nL84: GHASH is a great candidate for `clmad` because it’s heavily based on binary multiplication in \\(GF(2^{128})\\), modulo the irreducible polynomial \\(X^{128} + X^7 + X^2 + X + 1\\).\nL85: In the broader context of AES-GCM, GHASH computes the core authentication hash over all of the input data (i.e., ciphertext, additional authenticated data, and a final length block). GHASH splits the input into 128-bit blocks and, for each block, XORs it into a running accumulator and multiplies the accumulator by a hash key (H) — derived from encrypting a zero block with AES — in \\(GF(2^{128})\\).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - snarang181/zk-duel · GitHub (https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 438\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL17:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL18:       * 【8†Code ReviewManage code changes】\nL19:       * 【9†Code QualityEnforce quality at merge】\nL20:     * APPLICATION SECURITY\nL21:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityFind and fix vulnerabilities】\nL22:       * 【11†Code securitySecure your co