ilies, plus a diagnostic tier of 7 polynomials. Both sets are listed below.\nL215: \nL216:   * Fields. Four prime fields in Montgomery form with 32-bit limbs:\nL217: Width  | Prime  | Limbs\nL218: --- | --- | ---\nL219: 32  | 2^32 − 5  | 1\nL220: 64  | Goldilocks, 2^64 − 2^32 + 1  | 2\nL221: 128  | 2^127 − 1  | 4\nL222: 256  | BLS12-381 scalar field (255-bit prime)  | 8\nL223:   * Correctness gating. A configuration counts as a result only when both backends finish and their checksums agree. Both backends are also checked against a CPU big-integer reference computed from the canonical expressions in `src/zkduel/workloads.py`.\nL224:   * Measurements. Median wall-clock time per SumCheck run, GPU-kernel time from Nsight Systems, registers and occupancy, and Triton compile time.\nL225: \nL226: zkPHIRE constraints (`--zk-only` selects all 25; the aliases `zkphire_0` to `zkphire_24` are also accepted):\nL227: Family  | n  | Workloads\nL228: --- | --- | ---\nL229: Verifiable / Spartan  | 3  | `zk_verifiable_asics`, `zk_spartan_1`, `zk_spartan_2`\nL230: Halo2 curve checks  | 3  | `zk_witness_non_id`, `zk_witness_id_point_1`, `zk_witness_id_point_2`\nL231: Incomplete addition  | 2  | `zk_incomplete_add_1`, `zk_incomplete_add_2`\nL232: Complete addition  | 12  | `zk_complete_add_1` to `zk_complete_add_12`\nL233: HyperPlonk vanilla  | 2  | `zk_vanilla_zerocheck_hp`, `zk_vanilla_permcheck_hp`\nL234: HyperPlonk Jellyfish  | 2  | `zk_jellyfish_zerocheck_hp`, `zk_jellyfish_permcheck_hp`\nL235: Opening check  | 1  | `zk_opencheck`\nL236: Diagnostic tier:\nL237: \nL238: Workload  | Polynomial\nL239: --- | ---\nL240: `poly_a`  | `a`\nL241: `poly_ab`  | `a*b`\nL242: `poly_ab_plus_c`  | `a*b + c`\nL243: `poly_abc`  | `a*b*c`\nL244: `poly_aabbc`  | `a*a*b*b*c`\nL245: `poly_abc_plus_de`  | `a*b*c + d*e`\nL246: `poly_abcg_plus_deg`  | `a*b*c*g + d*e*g`\nL247: ## Repository layout\nL248: \nL249:     `src/zkduel/                    Python package: fields and modulus guard (fields.py), canonical\nL250:                                    workload expressions (workloads.py), CPU big-integer SumCheck\nL251:                                    reference (reference.py), 256-bit Triton backend (triton_backend/)\nL252:     benchmarks/field_sweep/        Prime-field drivers used for the paper:\nL253:       triton_sweep.py              Triton SumCheck for all 32 workloads and 4 widths\nL254:       compare_triton_cuda.py       Triton vs CUDA, joined CSV with checksum agreement\nL255:       resilient_compare_triton_cuda.py  Same, one subprocess per case, with a per-case timeout\nL256:       workload_specs.py            Workload metadata and aliases\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats/commits/main/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats/commits/main/: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.08258] zkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08258)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08258\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 6 Oct 2026 (【5†v1】), last revised 11 Oct 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:zkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Junkai Liang】, 【7†Zhanpeng Guo】, 【8†Pengfei Wu】, 【9†Qingni Shen】, 【10†Jiaheng Zhang】, 【11†Zhonghai Wu】, 【12†Haiyang Xue】, 【13†Shengfang Zhai】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled zkLLMPoT: Efficient Zero Knowledge Proof of Training for Large Language Models, by Junkai Liang and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: 【14†View PDF】 【15†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Auditing the claimed outcomes of large language model (LLM) training is challenging when model weights and training data are private, while cryptographically proving the full training process is prohibitively expensive at Transformer scale. We present zkLLMPoT, a zero-knowledge framework that certifies auditor-defined properties of a trained checkpoint through forward evaluation rather than verification of its optimization trajectory.\nL17: zkLLMPoT includes 2 phases: 1) The trainer fixes the architecture and the model weights are committed. Then the auditor selects challenge sequences, preventing the trainer from modifying the checkpoint in response to the audit data. 2) Then the trainer proves the objective value attained by the committed model on those sequences. This formulation makes the certification cost independent of the number of training iterations, without revealing model weights or requiring access to private training data.\nL18: We build on sumcheck- and lookup-based arguments to certify Transformer computations, while supporting next-token loss and task-specific audit objectives. Across four model families, operator-level benchmarks yield proving times of 41-59 seconds for 1.1-1.5B-parameter models and 131 seconds at 13B for the covered operators, with verification below half a second at a sequence length of 512.\nL19: Comments:  | 18 pages, 4 figures, Preprint\nL20: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL21: MSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 94A60, 68T50\nL22: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | E.3; I.2.6; I.2.7\nL23: Cite as:  | 【16†arXiv:2610.08258】 [cs.AI]\nL24:    | (or 【17†arXiv:2610.08258v2】 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL25:    | 【18†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.08258†doi.